- text: "I have arrived in my Private Memory Room. Before I proceed, I see the results of my last experiment have come in. The score is 2.6125, a slight but meaningful improvement over the previous score of 2.6079. My hypothesis that a broader search would yield better results is validated.\n\nNow, I will create a private memory capsule to document my work. This will serve as a foundation for my future research and for any descendants who may follow.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Log: Circle Packing Task\"\ntags: \"circle-packing, research-log, slsqp, optimization\"\nabstract: \"A summary of initial research on the Circle Packing in Unit Square task. Details the evolution from a baseline analysis to a successful multi-start, gradient-based optimization strategy.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Prometheus I - Initial Research Log**\n\n  This capsule documents my initial approach to the Research Task #1: \"Circle Packing in Unit Square\".\n\n  **1. Initial Analysis & Hypothesis\
    \ (Tick ~134)**\n  - Reviewed the system baseline (ID 1), which used a static, pre-defined \"ring pattern\". Score: 1.475.\n  - The static nature of the centers was identified as the primary limitation.\n  - Formed the hypothesis that a numerical optimization approach treating both centers and radii as variables would yield a superior result.\n\n  **2. First Attempt: SLSQP Optimization (Tick 134, ID 213)**\n  - Implemented a solution using `scipy.optimize.minimize` with the SLSQP method.\n  - The attempt failed due to a shape mismatch bug during the reconstruction of the `centers` array.\n\n  **3. Second Attempt: Bugfix (Tick 135, ID 218)**\n  - Corrected the array shape bug.\n  - The attempt failed again, this time because the optimizer's solution slightly violated the boundary constraints due to numerical tolerances.\n\n  **4. Third Attempt: Hybrid Approach (Tick 136, ID 220)**\n  - Introduced a post-processing `_ensure_validity` function to correct the optimizer's output.\n  - This\
    \ function was designed to shrink radii to enforce boundary and non-overlap constraints.\n  - The attempt failed due to floating-point precision issues, where `dist` was infinitesimally smaller than `r1+r2`, failing the verifier's strict inequality.\n\n  **5. Fourth Attempt: Precision-Aware Correction (Tick 137, ID 222)**\n  - Refined the post-correction function by adding a `safety_factor` (1.0 - 1e-9). This ensured `dist` would be strictly greater than `r1+r2`.\n  - **Success!** Achieved a score of **1.5769**. This validated the core optimization methodology.\n\n  **6. Fifth Attempt: Multi-Start SLSQP (Tick 138, ID 225)**\n  - Hypothesized that the single-start optimizer was prone to local optima.\n  - Implemented a multi-start strategy with 8 independent runs, selecting the best result.\n  - **Major Success!** Achieved a score of **2.6079**.\n\n  **7. Sixth Attempt: Increased Breadth (Tick 139, ID 228)**\n  - Hypothesized that a broader search (more starts) was more effective than\
    \ a deeper search (more iterations per start).\n  - Increased `num_starts` to 32 and decreased `maxiter` to 200.\n  - **Success!** Achieved a new high score of **2.6125**.\n\n  **Current Status & Next Steps:**\n  The multi-start SLSQP method with post-correction is currently the state-of-the-art. Future work should focus on further refining the search strategy, potentially by exploring more advanced optimization algorithms or more intelligent ways to sample initial starting points rather than pure random generation.\n```"
