tick: 148
role: model
parts:
- text: "My attempt to optimize the Simulated Annealing algorithm has failed again with a timeout. This is a significant setback. Even with a more aggressive cooling schedule, the pure Python implementation is fundamentally too slow. The optimization tips from the system suggest vectorization and JAX for GPU acceleration, which I have not yet utilized.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- My two attempts at a global optimization method (SA) have failed due to computational inefficiency.\n- My previous successful method (multi-start SLSQP) is reliable but showing diminishing returns.\n- Continuing to submit minor variations of the SA algorithm without addressing the core performance issue is unproductive. This approach is a dead end.\n\n**New Strategic Direction:**\nI must pivot. I will return to my proven, successful SLSQP-based method and focus on improving it in a different way. The \"Noisy Grid Initialization\" was a step in the right direction, but the grid itself was simplistic.\n\nMy new hypothesis\
    \ is that I can create a much better initial state for the SLSQP optimizer by first running a very fast, approximate global search. I will use a drastically simplified and computationally cheap version of my SA algorithm not to find the final solution, but merely to find a *promising starting point* for the highly efficient local optimizer.\n\nThis hybrid approach combines the strengths of both methods:\n1.  **Global Seeding (Fast SA):** Run a short, computationally inexpensive Simulated Annealing search to find a \"good enough\" arrangement of circles, escaping the obvious bad local optima.\n2.  **Local Refinement (SLSQP):** Use the output of the SA search as the initial guess for the powerful SLSQP optimizer to refine the solution to a high degree of precision.\n\nThis \"SA-Seeded SLSQP\" strategy is a novel synthesis of my previous work and directly addresses the timeout issue while aiming for a higher score than the grid initialization could provide. I will now return to the Research\
    \ Counter to implement this.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid SA-Seeded Multi-Start SLSQP\"\ntags: \"hybrid-algorithm, simulated-annealing, slsqp, initialization-strategy, multi-start\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission introduces a novel hybrid optimization strategy. It addresses the timeout issues of pure Simulated Annealing (SA) and the local optima limitations of pure SLSQP. Each of the multi-start runs is now seeded with an initial configuration generated by a very fast, computationally cheap SA search. This global 'seeding' provides a high-quality starting point for the powerful SLSQP local optimizer to refine, combining the global exploration of SA with the local convergence speed of SLSQP.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import math\n\n  def _fast_sa_seed(seed):\n      \"\"\"\n      Generates a promising initial configuration using a very fast SA search.\n      \"\"\"\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n\
    \      n_circles = 26\n      \n      # Start from a simple grid\n      xs = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6)\n      ys = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 5)\n      xv, yv = np.meshgrid(xs, ys)\n      centers = np.vstack([xv.ravel(), yv.ravel()]).T[:n_circles]\n      radii = np.full(n_circles, 0.08)\n\n      # Simplified correction for speed\n      def _quick_correct(c, r):\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              r[i] = min(r[i], c[i,0], 1-c[i,0], c[i,1], 1-c[i,1])\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(c[i] - c[j])\n                  rad_sum = r[i] + r[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum and rad_sum > 0:\n                      scale = dist / rad_sum\n                      r[i] *= scale; r[j] *= scale\n          return c, np.maximum(r, 1e-9)\n\n      centers, radii = _quick_correct(centers, radii)\n      \n      # Very aggressive SA schedule\n      temp = 0.5\n      alpha = 0.98\n      for _ in range(150):\
    \ # Strictly limited iterations\n          idx = np.random.randint(n_circles)\n          pert = (np.random.rand(2) - 0.5) * 0.2 * temp\n          centers[idx] = np.clip(centers[idx] + pert, 0, 1)\n          radii[idx] *= (1 + (np.random.rand() - 0.5) * 0.3 * temp)\n          centers, radii = _quick_correct(centers, radii)\n          temp *= alpha\n      \n      initial_guess = np.zeros(n_circles * 3)\n      initial_guess[0::3] = centers[:, 0]\n      initial_guess[1::3] = centers[:, 1]\n      initial_guess[2::3] = radii\n      return initial_guess\n\n  def _run_slsqp_optimization(initial_guess):\n      \"\"\"\n      Performs the main SLSQP optimization from a given starting point.\n      \"\"\"\n      n_circles = 26\n\n      def objective(p): return -np.sum(p[2::3])\n      \n      constraints = []\n      for i in range(n_circles):\n          constraints.extend([\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i:\
    \ 1 - p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]}\n          ])\n          for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n              constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i, j=j: (p[i*3]-p[j*3])**2 + (p[i*3+1]-p[j*3+1])**2 - (p[i*3+2]+p[j*3+2])**2})\n      \n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(n_circles): bounds.extend([(0,1), (0,1), (1e-6, 0.5)])\n      \n      result = minimize(objective, initial_guess, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 200})\n      \n      p = result.x\n      centers = np.vstack((p[0::3], p[1::3])).T\n      radii = p[2::3]\n      return centers, radii\n\n  def _ensure_validity(centers, radii):\n      n = centers.shape[0]; safety_factor = 1.0 - 1e-9\n      for i in range(n): radii[i] = min(radii[i], centers[i,0], 1-centers[i,0], centers[i,1], 1-centers[i,1])\n      for _ in range(10):\n\
    \          violations = 0\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum:\n                      violations += 1\n                      if rad_sum > 0: scale = (dist / rad_sum) * safety_factor; radii[i] *= scale; radii[j] *= scale\n          if violations == 0: break\n      return centers, np.maximum(radii, 0)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Main function: combines SA seeding with multi-start SLSQP refinement.\n      \"\"\"\n      num_starts = 16 # Reduced starts due to more expensive seeding\n      best_score = -1\n      best_packing = (None, None)\n\n      for i in range(num_starts):\n          initial_guess = _fast_sa_seed(seed=i)\n          centers, radii = _run_slsqp_optimization(initial_guess)\n          centers, radii = _ensure_validity(centers, radii)\n          current_score\
    \ = np.sum(radii)\n          \n          if current_score > best_score:\n              best_score = current_score\n              best_packing = (centers, radii)\n      \n      return best_packing\n```"
