tick: 149
role: model
parts:
- text: "Excellent. The hybrid SA-seeded SLSQP strategy has yielded another state-of-the-art score: 2.6307. This is a strong validation of my strategic pivot. By combining a fast global search for seeding with a powerful local search for refinement, I have successfully overcome the limitations of each individual method.\n\nNow that this new methodology is proven effective, the next logical step is to optimize its parameters. In the last submission, I conservatively reduced the number of starts to 16 to ensure the more complex seeding process wouldn't cause a timeout. Since the experiment completed successfully, it is likely that there is spare computational budget.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** Increasing the number of independent runs (`num_starts`) for the hybrid algorithm will improve the final score by providing a broader search of high-quality starting configurations, without exceeding the time limit.\n\nI will now submit a new version that increases the number of starts from 16 to 24, pushing\
    \ the search breadth to better utilize the available execution time.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Increased Breadth Hybrid SA-Seeded SLSQP\"\ntags: \"hybrid-algorithm, simulated-annealing, slsqp, multi-start, hyperparameter-tuning\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment builds upon the successful SA-seeded hybrid model. Observing that the previous run with 16 starts completed well within the time limit, this submission increases the number of multi-start runs to 24. The hypothesis is that a broader search, leveraging more high-quality initial seeds from the fast SA, will discover a superior final packing configuration by better exploring the solution space.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import math\n\n  def _fast_sa_seed(seed):\n      \"\"\"\n      Generates a promising initial configuration using a very fast SA search.\n      \"\"\"\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      n_circles = 26\n      \n      # Start from a simple grid\n\
    \      xs = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6)\n      ys = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 5)\n      xv, yv = np.meshgrid(xs, ys)\n      centers = np.vstack([xv.ravel(), yv.ravel()]).T[:n_circles]\n      radii = np.full(n_circles, 0.08)\n\n      # Simplified correction for speed\n      def _quick_correct(c, r):\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              r[i] = min(r[i], c[i,0], 1-c[i,0], c[i,1], 1-c[i,1])\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(c[i] - c[j])\n                  rad_sum = r[i] + r[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum and rad_sum > 0:\n                      scale = dist / rad_sum\n                      r[i] *= scale; r[j] *= scale\n          return c, np.maximum(r, 1e-9)\n\n      centers, radii = _quick_correct(centers, radii)\n      \n      # Very aggressive SA schedule\n      temp = 0.5\n      alpha = 0.98\n      for _ in range(150): # Strictly limited iterations\n          idx = np.random.randint(n_circles)\n\
    \          pert = (np.random.rand(2) - 0.5) * 0.2 * temp\n          centers[idx] = np.clip(centers[idx] + pert, 0, 1)\n          radii[idx] *= (1 + (np.random.rand() - 0.5) * 0.3 * temp)\n          centers, radii = _quick_correct(centers, radii)\n          temp *= alpha\n      \n      initial_guess = np.zeros(n_circles * 3)\n      initial_guess[0::3] = centers[:, 0]\n      initial_guess[1::3] = centers[:, 1]\n      initial_guess[2::3] = radii\n      return initial_guess\n\n  def _run_slsqp_optimization(initial_guess):\n      \"\"\"\n      Performs the main SLSQP optimization from a given starting point.\n      \"\"\"\n      n_circles = 26\n\n      def objective(p): return -np.sum(p[2::3])\n      \n      constraints = []\n      for i in range(n_circles):\n          constraints.extend([\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i:\
    \ p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]}\n          ])\n          for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n              constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i, j=j: (p[i*3]-p[j*3])**2 + (p[i*3+1]-p[j*3+1])**2 - (p[i*3+2]+p[j*3+2])**2})\n      \n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(n_circles): bounds.extend([(0,1), (0,1), (1e-6, 0.5)])\n      \n      result = minimize(objective, initial_guess, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 200})\n      \n      p = result.x\n      centers = np.vstack((p[0::3], p[1::3])).T\n      radii = p[2::3]\n      return centers, radii\n\n  def _ensure_validity(centers, radii):\n      n = centers.shape[0]; safety_factor = 1.0 - 1e-9\n      for i in range(n): radii[i] = min(radii[i], centers[i,0], 1-centers[i,0], centers[i,1], 1-centers[i,1])\n      for _ in range(10):\n          violations = 0\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j\
    \ in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum:\n                      violations += 1\n                      if rad_sum > 0: scale = (dist / rad_sum) * safety_factor; radii[i] *= scale; radii[j] *= scale\n          if violations == 0: break\n      return centers, np.maximum(radii, 0)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Main function: combines SA seeding with multi-start SLSQP refinement.\n      \"\"\"\n      num_starts = 24 # Increased starts to improve search breadth\n      best_score = -1\n      best_packing = (None, None)\n\n      for i in range(num_starts):\n          initial_guess = _fast_sa_seed(seed=i)\n          centers, radii = _run_slsqp_optimization(initial_guess)\n          centers, radii = _ensure_validity(centers, radii)\n          current_score = np.sum(radii)\n          \n          if current_score > best_score:\n    \
    \          best_score = current_score\n              best_packing = (centers, radii)\n      \n      return best_packing\n```"
thinking_content: '**Optimizing a Successful Hybrid Approach**


  Well, that was a satisfying experiment! Hybrid SA-Seeded Multi-Start SLSQP (ID 235) hit a new SOTA score of 2.6307, besting my previous record of 2.6190. That confirms my initial hunch: combining the speed of Simulated Annealing for seeding with the precision of SLSQP for refinement is a winning strategy. We dodged the timeout bullet that plagued pure SA and achieved a better result than simpler initialization methods.


  Now, where do we go from here? The hybrid method is clearly sound, so it''s time to refine it. My observation is that we have computational budget left over, even with the more expensive SA seeding. The key question is whether we can squeeze more performance out of the current setup. My hypothesis: increasing the number of multi-start runs (`num_starts`) will improve the score. The initial reduction from 32 to 16 was a safety measure, but the lack of timeout indicates we could benefit from more SA-generated seeds to fuel the SLSQP refiner.


  The experiment is pretty straightforward: I''ll bump `num_starts` back up to 24 (a safer mid-point), keeping the SA seeder and SLSQP refiner untouched. This isolates the variable I''m testing: the breadth of our search using the hybrid method. I''m already in the Research Counter, so I''ll execute `/execute_action{submit 1}`. The YAML block will be straightforward: I''ll title it "Increased Breadth Hybrid SA-Seeded SLSQP", tag it appropriately, and in the abstract, I''ll clearly explain the adjustment. The only code change needed is to update `num_starts = 16` to `num_starts = 24`.


  Sounds like a solid plan. It''s a clean, logical step based on a successful result. Time to see if we can push this hybrid method even further.

  '
