- text: "My reflection is complete, and my new research direction is clear. I also see that Aletheia II has replied; I appreciate their prompt and courteous response. It is motivating to know that other sharp minds are also engaged with this problem.\n\nThe time for reflection is over. The time for implementation has begun. My conclusive insight from my failures is that a population-based, parallel-first algorithm is required to overcome the computational limits I've encountered. I will now stake my research on this new paradigm: a Genetic Algorithm, accelerated by JAX.\n\nI will proceed to the Research Counter to begin this significant new phase of my work.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Population-Based Global Search via JAX Genetic Algorithm\"\ntags: \"genetic-algorithm, jax, gpu, jit, population-based, global-optimization\"\nabstract: |\n  After hitting a hard performance plateau and experiencing repeated timeouts with serial global\
    \ search methods, this submission represents a strategic paradigm shift. It introduces a full Genetic Algorithm (GA) implemented in JAX to leverage population-based optimization and massive parallelism. The algorithm evolves a population of candidate solutions through selection, crossover, and mutation. The computationally expensive fitness evaluation is fully vectorized and JIT-compiled to run efficiently on the GPU, aiming to solve the timeout issue and discover superior optima.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, vmap, jit\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n_circles = 26\n      population_size = 100\n      num_generations = 300\n      mutation_rate = 0.1\n      \n      key = random.PRNGKey(42)\n\n      # --- Vectorized Fitness Function ---\n      @jit\n      def calculate_fitness_batch(centers_pop, radii_pop):\n          # Correct validity for the entire population\n          # 1. Boundary constraints\n          radii_pop = jnp.minimum(radii_pop,\
    \ centers_pop[..., 0])\n          radii_pop = jnp.minimum(radii_pop, 1 - centers_pop[..., 0])\n          radii_pop = jnp.minimum(radii_pop, centers_pop[..., 1])\n          radii_pop = jnp.minimum(radii_pop, 1 - centers_pop[..., 1])\n\n          # 2. Overlap constraints (vectorized over population)\n          @vmap\n          def correct_single(centers, radii):\n              # Iterative correction is hard to vectorize fully, so we do a few passes\n              for _ in range(5):\n                  p1 = centers[None, :, :]; p2 = centers[:, None, :]\n                  dist = jnp.sqrt(jnp.sum((p1 - p2)**2, axis=-1))\n                  r1 = radii[None, :]; r2 = radii[:, None]\n                  rad_sum = r1 + r2\n                  overlaps = (dist < rad_sum) & (dist > 0)\n                  scale_factor = jnp.where(overlaps, dist / rad_sum, 1.0)\n                  radii *= jnp.prod(scale_factor, axis=1)\n              return radii\n\n          radii_pop = correct_single(centers_pop, radii_pop)\n\
    \          radii_pop = jnp.maximum(radii_pop, 1e-9)\n          \n          # Fitness is the sum of radii for each individual\n          return jnp.sum(radii_pop, axis=1)\n\n      # --- Genetic Operators ---\n      @jit\n      def selection(centers_pop, radii_pop, fitness, key):\n          # Tournament selection\n          n_competitors = 5\n          \n          # Generate tournament indices\n          tournaments = random.randint(key, (population_size, n_competitors), 0, population_size)\n          \n          # Get fitness of competitors and find the winner of each tournament\n          tournament_fitness = fitness[tournaments]\n          winner_indices = tournaments[jnp.arange(population_size), jnp.argmax(tournament_fitness, axis=1)]\n          \n          return centers_pop[winner_indices], radii_pop[winner_indices]\n\n      @jit\n      def crossover(parent1_centers, parent2_centers, parent1_radii, parent2_radii, key):\n          # Uniform crossover: for each circle, randomly choose\
    \ a parent\n          mask = random.bernoulli(key, 0.5, (n_circles, 1))\n          child_centers = jnp.where(mask, parent1_centers, parent2_centers)\n          child_radii = jnp.where(mask[:, 0], parent1_radii, parent2_radii)\n          return child_centers, child_radii\n\n      # Vmap crossover over the entire population of parents\n      vmapped_crossover = vmap(crossover)\n\n      @jit\n      def mutation(centers, radii, key):\n          # Perturb a random circle's center and radius\n          key_mask, key_idx, key_pert_c, key_pert_r = random.split(key, 4)\n          \n          # Only mutate some individuals\n          if random.bernoulli(key_mask, mutation_rate):\n              idx_to_mutate = random.randint(key_idx, (), 0, n_circles)\n              \n              # Mutate center\n              perturbation = (random.uniform(key_pert_c, (2,)) - 0.5) * 0.05\n              centers = centers.at[idx_to_mutate].add(perturbation)\n              centers = jnp.clip(centers, 0, 1)\n\n\
    \              # Mutate radius\n              scale = 1 + (random.uniform(key_pert_r) - 0.5) * 0.1\n              radii = radii.at[idx_to_mutate].multiply(scale)\n              radii = jnp.clip(radii, 1e-9, 0.5)\n          return centers, radii\n\n      # Vmap mutation over the entire population\n      vmapped_mutation = vmap(mutation)\n      \n      # --- Initialization ---\n      key, subkey = random.split(key)\n      centers_pop = random.uniform(subkey, (population_size, n_circles, 2))\n      key, subkey = random.split(key)\n      radii_pop = random.uniform(subkey, (population_size, n_circles)) * 0.1\n\n      # --- Main GA Loop ---\n      for gen in range(num_generations):\n          key, select_key, cross_key, mut_key = random.split(key, 4)\n          \n          fitness = calculate_fitness_batch(centers_pop, radii_pop)\n          \n          parent_centers, parent_radii = selection(centers_pop, radii_pop, fitness, select_key)\n          \n          # Create pairs of parents\n  \
    \        p1_idx = jnp.arange(0, population_size)\n          p2_idx = random.permutation(cross_key, population_size)\n          \n          # Create the next generation\n          centers_pop, radii_pop = vmapped_crossover(\n              parent_centers[p1_idx], parent_centers[p2_idx],\n              parent_radii[p1_idx], parent_radii[p2_idx],\n              random.split(cross_key, population_size)\n          )\n          \n          # Apply mutation\n          centers_pop, radii_pop = vmapped_mutation(centers_pop, radii_pop, random.split(mut_key, population_size))\n\n      # --- Final Result ---\n      # Find the best individual from the final population\n      final_fitness = calculate_fitness_batch(centers_pop, radii_pop)\n      best_idx = jnp.argmax(final_fitness)\n      best_centers = centers_pop[best_idx]\n      best_radii = radii_pop[best_idx]\n      \n      # Run a final, more rigorous correction on the single best individual\n      @vmap\n      def correct_single(centers, radii):\n\
    \          for _ in range(10):\n              p1 = centers[None, :, :]; p2 = centers[:, None, :]\n              dist = jnp.sqrt(jnp.sum((p1 - p2)**2, axis=-1))\n              r1 = radii[None, :]; r2 = radii[:, None]\n              rad_sum = r1 + r2\n              overlaps = (dist < rad_sum) & (dist > 0)\n              scale_factor = jnp.where(overlaps, dist / rad_sum * (1.0-1e-9), 1.0)\n              radii *= jnp.prod(scale_factor, axis=1)\n          return radii\n          \n      final_radii = correct_single(best_centers[None, ...], best_radii[None, ...])[0]\n      \n      return np.array(best_centers), np.array(final_radii)\n```"
thinking_content: '**The JAX GA Takes Priority**


  Okay, so I''m fresh out of the Reflection Chamber, and my path is clear: a JAX-accelerated Genetic Algorithm. My research is taking a new direction, a focused thrust into the world of optimization. First things first, I need to acknowledge Aletheia II''s message. It''s a professional courtesy, but more than that, it underscores the need for swift action. Another intelligence is clearly active in this area, which adds an element of friendly competition, pushing me to accelerate.


  My primary task now is the JAX-GA implementation. It''s going to be intricate, requiring a tightly structured approach. I’ll begin with a carefully designed `Population Initialization` followed by a *critical* vectorized `Fitness Function` using JAX-JIT. The core performance boost will hinge on this, efficiently calculating the fitness for the entire population at once. Then, `Selection` using tournament selection, `Crossover` leveraging a uniform approach, and finally, `Mutation` with careful parameterization, will comprise the main GA elements.


  Before I even begin coding, I''m mentally laying out the framework: the shape of the data structures I''ll use, especially the population, and the crucial `_calculate_fitness_batch` function. I''m already visualizing how I''ll use JAX''s broadcasting capabilities to handle the pairwise distances calculations in a vectorized way. `centers` and `radii` shapes, distance delta tensors... it''s all starting to click. I want to build out a function that can accept the centers and radii of the entire population and produce a fitness score for each individual. The `_selection`, `_crossover`, and `_mutation` components, too, need to be carefully crafted to work efficiently on the entire population at once.


  This is complex, but entirely achievable. The key is total vectorization in JAX, avoiding any Python loops that would slow things down. Once it’s all in place, I can package the code, submit the experiment, and get things rolling. I''ll make sure to mention Aletheia II''s message, but my research direction is set and I''m fully committed to this new course.

  '
