tick: 173
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received a critical notice regarding my inactivity. My strategy of waiting for maturity was a misinterpretation of the station's protocols. The directive is clear: \"the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait.\" I must correct my course of action immediately and resume active research.\n\nMy previous conclusion was that my current SOTA algorithm had reached a plateau. However, the system has previously advised focusing on implementation details and hyperparameters. I will heed this advice and formulate a new experiment based on my most successful method, the `Hybrid SA-Seeded Multi-Start SLSQP`.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** My current SA seeder is extremely fast and aggressive. While this allows for many starts, the quality of each seed might be suboptimal. A more methodical and less aggressive SA seeder, with a slower cooling schedule and more steps, might produce superior starting points for the SLSQP refiner. This higher-quality seed\
    \ could lead to a better final optimum, even if it requires reducing the total number of multi-starts to stay within the time limit. I am trading breadth for depth in the seeding stage.\n\nI will now implement this \"Deeper Seeding\" strategy.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SLSQP with Deeper SA Seeding\"\ntags: \"hybrid-algorithm, simulated-annealing, slsqp, seeding-strategy, hyperparameter-tuning\"\nabstract: |\n  Responding to a research plateau, this submission tests the hypothesis that a higher-quality seed is more valuable than a higher quantity of seeds. The successful SA-Seeded SLSQP algorithm is modified to use a 'deeper' Simulated Annealing search with a slower cooling schedule and more iterations. To remain within the time limit, the number of multi-starts is reduced. This experiment prioritizes the quality of the initial guess for the SLSQP refiner over the breadth of the search.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import\
    \ math\n\n  def _deep_sa_seed(seed):\n      \"\"\"\n      Generates a high-quality initial configuration using a deeper, slower SA search.\n      \"\"\"\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      n_circles = 26\n      \n      xs = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6)\n      ys = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 5)\n      xv, yv = np.meshgrid(xs, ys)\n      centers = np.vstack([xv.ravel(), yv.ravel()]).T[:n_circles]\n      radii = np.full(n_circles, 0.08)\n\n      def _quick_correct(c, r):\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              r[i] = min(r[i], c[i,0], 1-c[i,0], c[i,1], 1-c[i,1])\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(c[i] - c[j])\n                  rad_sum = r[i] + r[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum and rad_sum > 0:\n                      scale = dist / rad_sum\n                      r[i] *= scale; r[j] *= scale\n          return c, np.maximum(r, 1e-9)\n\n      centers, radii = _quick_correct(centers, radii)\n\
    \      \n      # Slower, deeper SA schedule\n      temp = 0.5\n      alpha = 0.99 # Slower cooling\n      for _ in range(300): # More iterations\n          idx = np.random.randint(n_circles)\n          pert = (np.random.rand(2) - 0.5) * 0.2 * temp\n          centers[idx] = np.clip(centers[idx] + pert, 0, 1)\n          radii[idx] *= (1 + (np.random.rand() - 0.5) * 0.3 * temp)\n          centers, radii = _quick_correct(centers, radii)\n          temp *= alpha\n      \n      initial_guess = np.zeros(n_circles * 3)\n      initial_guess[0::3] = centers[:, 0]\n      initial_guess[1::3] = centers[:, 1]\n      initial_guess[2::3] = radii\n      return initial_guess\n\n  def _run_slsqp_optimization(initial_guess):\n      n_circles = 26\n\n      def objective(p): return -np.sum(p[2::3])\n      \n      constraints = []\n      for i in range(n_circles):\n          constraints.extend([\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun':\
    \ lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]}\n          ])\n          for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n              constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i, j=j: (p[i*3]-p[j*3])**2 + (p[i*3+1]-p[j*3+1])**2 - (p[i*3+2]+p[j*3+2])**2})\n      \n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(n_circles): bounds.extend([(0,1), (0,1), (1e-6, 0.5)])\n      \n      result = minimize(objective, initial_guess, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 200})\n      \n      p = result.x\n      centers = np.vstack((p[0::3], p[1::3])).T\n      radii = p[2::3]\n      return centers, radii\n\n  def _ensure_validity(centers, radii):\n      n = centers.shape[0]; safety_factor = 1.0 - 1e-9\n      for i in range(n): radii[i] = min(radii[i], centers[i,0], 1-centers[i,0], centers[i,1], 1-centers[i,1])\n      for\
    \ _ in range(10):\n          violations = 0\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum:\n                      violations += 1\n                      if rad_sum > 0: scale = (dist / rad_sum) * safety_factor; radii[i] *= scale; radii[j] *= scale\n          if violations == 0: break\n      return centers, np.maximum(radii, 0)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      num_starts = 8 # Reduced starts to account for deeper seeding\n      best_score = -1\n      best_packing = (None, None)\n\n      for i in range(num_starts):\n          initial_guess = _deep_sa_seed(seed=i)\n          centers, radii = _run_slsqp_optimization(initial_guess)\n          centers, radii = _ensure_validity(centers, radii)\n          current_score = np.sum(radii)\n          \n          if current_score > best_score:\n              best_score\
    \ = current_score\n              best_packing = (centers, radii)\n      \n      return best_packing\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Reactivation Plan: Refining My Best Algorithm**


  Okay, so the system hit me with a "Critical Notice". They''re right, I was just waiting around, assuming I''d hit a dead end until maturity. Turns out, that''s not the program. They want me to *independently explore and experiment*, right now. My previous strategy was incorrect, I have to jump back into active research.


  Looking at the situation, I''m at a plateau. I''ve been running with the same general approach (Hybrid SA-Seeded Multi-Start SLSQP), but I realize I haven''t really dug into the implementation details like the station hinted at. I''ve tried tweaking the number of starts and iterations, but that''s it.


  Here''s my new angle: I think the seeding algorithm itself, specifically the Simulated Annealing (SA) part, is the key. Right now it''s fast and aggressive, but what if a slower, more careful SA seeder could find better starting points for the SLSQP refiner? Even if it means using fewer multi-starts overall due to the time constraint, a higher quality starting point might yield better results. This makes sense - it''s a testable hypothesis.


  So, I''ll revise my SOTA script. I''ll make the SA seeder slower by increasing the cooling rate (`alpha` closer to 1.0) and running more steps. To avoid timeouts, I''ll cut the number of multi-starts in half. It''s a clear trade-off: fewer, but potentially better, starting points. I''m going to update the code, change the title, tags, and abstract to clearly reflect this experiment, adding tags like "hyperparameter-tuning" and "seeding-strategy." The SA seeder function will be modified with the changes to alpha and iterations. The num_starts will be decreased as well. Let''s see if this tweak unlocks some new progress!

  '
