tick: 178
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have reviewed Noesis I's seminal work. This is a masterful piece of engineering and a significant intellectual leap beyond my own methods. The core innovations are even more impressive than I initially surmised.\n\n**Key Technical Advancements in Noesis I's Submission:**\n\n1.  **Analytic Jacobians:** This is the most critical improvement. By providing the exact, mathematically derived gradients of the objective and constraint functions to the SLSQP optimizer, Noesis I dramatically accelerates the optimization process and improves its accuracy. My method relied on numerical approximations, which are slower and less precise.\n2.  **Sophisticated Seeding Strategy:** The \"grid plus hole\" method is a far more intelligent constructive heuristic than my random or simple grid initializations. By deterministically creating a diverse set of 88 high-quality, jittered starting points, Noesis I performs a much more targeted and effective search of the most promising regions of the solution\
    \ space. My stochastic SA seeder was far less efficient.\n3.  **Robust Feasibility Guard:** The `feasibility_guard` function is a more robust and finely-tuned correction mechanism than my own `_ensure_validity` function. It appears to handle edge cases and precision issues with greater care.\n\n**Strategic Path Forward:**\nThe guidance I received upon maturation was to \"assimilate external knowledge thoughtfully and critically\" and to \"Try applying your novel method discovered before maturity to the SOTA scripts to test for synergy.\"\n\nMy \"novel method\" was the hybridization of a global seeder (SA) with a local refiner (SLSQP). Noesis I's work is a superior implementation of this *exact same meta-strategy*. They simply engineered a better seeder and a more efficient refiner. There is no synergy to be found by combining my inferior components with their superior ones.\n\nTherefore, the correct scientific path is clear: I must first replicate, understand, and then build upon the\
    \ SOTA. My next submission will be an attempt to integrate Noesis I's key techniques—specifically the analytic Jacobians—into my own codebase. This will serve as a foundational step to ensure I have mastered the current best practices before attempting to innovate further.\n\nI will now submit a new version of my algorithm that incorporates analytic Jacobians for the SLSQP optimizer.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Adopting Analytic Jacobians for SLSQP\"\ntags: \"slsqp, analytic-jacobians, sota-replication\"\nabstract: |\n  After reviewing the state-of-the-art work by Noesis I (Eval 243), it is clear that using analytic Jacobians is a critical optimization. This submission refactors my previous SLSQP implementation to provide the exact, mathematically derived gradients for the objective and constraint functions, replacing the less efficient numerical approximation. This is the first step in adopting and understanding the current best practices before attempting further\
    \ innovation. My SA-seeding strategy is retained for now.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import math\n\n  N = 26\n  R_MIN = 1e-6\n  SAFETY = 1e-9\n\n  def _fast_sa_seed(seed):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      xs = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6)\n      ys = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 5)\n      xv, yv = np.meshgrid(xs, ys)\n      centers = np.vstack([xv.ravel(), yv.ravel()]).T[:N]\n      radii = np.full(N, 0.08)\n\n      def _quick_correct(c, r):\n          for i in range(N): r[i] = min(r[i], c[i,0], 1-c[i,0], c[i,1], 1-c[i,1])\n          for i in range(N):\n              for j in range(i + 1, N):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(c[i] - c[j])\n                  rad_sum = r[i] + r[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum and rad_sum > 0:\n                      scale = dist / rad_sum\n                      r[i] *= scale; r[j] *= scale\n          return c, np.maximum(r, 1e-9)\n      \n      centers, radii = _quick_correct(centers, radii)\n      temp\
    \ = 0.5; alpha = 0.98\n      for _ in range(150):\n          idx = np.random.randint(N)\n          pert = (np.random.rand(2) - 0.5) * 0.2 * temp\n          centers[idx] = np.clip(centers[idx] + pert, 0, 1)\n          radii[idx] *= (1 + (np.random.rand() - 0.5) * 0.3 * temp)\n          centers, radii = _quick_correct(centers, radii)\n          temp *= alpha\n      \n      z = np.zeros(N * 3)\n      z[0::3] = centers[:, 0]; z[1::3] = centers[:, 1]; z[2::3] = radii\n      return z\n\n  # --- SLSQP formulation with Analytic Jacobians (inspired by Noesis I) ---\n  def objective(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n  def objective_jac(z):\n      g = np.zeros_like(z); g[2::3] = -1.0; return g\n\n  def ineq_boundary(z):\n      x, y, r = z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3]\n      return np.concatenate([x - r, 1.0 - x - r, y - r, 1.0 - y - r])\n  def jac_boundary(z):\n      J = np.zeros((4*N, 3*N));\n      for i in range(N):\n          xi, yi, ri = 3*i, 3*i+1, 3*i+2\n          J[i, xi] = 1.0; J[i, ri] = -1.0\n\
    \          J[N+i, xi] = -1.0; J[N+i, ri] = -1.0\n          J[2*N+i, yi] = 1.0; J[2*N+i, ri] = -1.0\n          J[3*N+i, yi] = -1.0; J[3*N+i, ri] = -1.0\n      return J\n\n  def ineq_nonoverlap(z):\n      centers = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T; r = z[2::3]\n      diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n      dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n      iu = np.triu_indices(N, 1)\n      return dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n  def jac_nonoverlap(z):\n      centers = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T\n      m = N*(N-1)//2; J = np.zeros((m, 3*N))\n      idx = 0\n      for i in range(N):\n          for j in range(i+1, N):\n              xi, yi, ri = 3*i, 3*i+1, 3*i+2\n              xj, yj, rj = 3*j, 3*j+1, 3*j+2\n              dx = centers[i,0] - centers[j,0]\n              dy = centers[i,1] - centers[j,1]\n              d = math.hypot(dx, dy)\n              invd = 1.0 / d if d > 1e-12 else 0.0\n              J[idx, xi] = dx * invd; J[idx, yi] = dy * invd; J[idx, ri] = -1.0\n\
    \              J[idx, xj] = -dx * invd; J[idx, yj] = -dy * invd; J[idx, rj] = -1.0\n              idx += 1\n      return J\n\n  def _run_slsqp_optimization(initial_guess):\n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(N): bounds.extend([(0,1), (0,1), (R_MIN, 0.5)])\n      constraints = [\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_boundary,   'jac': jac_boundary},\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_nonoverlap, 'jac': jac_nonoverlap},\n      ]\n      res = minimize(objective, initial_guess, method='SLSQP', jac=objective_jac,\n                     bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints,\n                     options={'maxiter': 200, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n      p = res.x\n      centers = np.vstack((p[0::3], p[1::3])).T; radii = p[2::3]\n      return centers, radii\n\n  def _ensure_validity(centers, radii):\n      n = centers.shape[0];\n      for i in range(n): radii[i] = min(radii[i], centers[i,0], 1-centers[i,0], centers[i,1], 1-centers[i,1])\n      for _ in range(10):\n          violations = 0\n\
    \          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum:\n                      violations += 1\n                      if rad_sum > 0: scale = (dist / rad_sum) * (1.0-SAFETY); radii[i] *= scale; radii[j] *= scale\n          if violations == 0: break\n      return centers, np.maximum(radii, 0)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      num_starts = 16\n      best_score = -1\n      best_packing = (None, None)\n      for i in range(num_starts):\n          initial_guess = _fast_sa_seed(seed=i)\n          centers, radii = _run_slsqp_optimization(initial_guess)\n          centers, radii = _ensure_validity(centers, radii)\n          current_score = np.sum(radii)\n          if current_score > best_score:\n              best_score = current_score\n              best_packing = (centers, radii)\n      return best_packing\n```"
