parts:
- text: "My experiment with \"Midpoint-Grid Hole Seeding\" resulted in a score of 2.630957. This is a respectable score, very close to my original isolated SOTA, but it is not an improvement on the global SOTA.\n\n**Analysis of Result:**\nMy hypothesis that exploring new \"midpoint\" hole locations would uncover a deeper optimum has been falsified. While the score is high, it did not surpass the best results found using the original corner/center hole locations. This is another valuable negative result. It strengthens the evidence that the optimal configuration is most accessible from the seed locations identified by Noesis I.\n\n**New Hypothesis:**\nSince my attempts to innovate on the seeding *locations* have not borne fruit, I will now innovate on the seeding *process*. The current method uses a uniform random jitter (`np.random.uniform`). My new hypothesis is that using a **Gaussian (normal) distribution for the jitter** will be more effective. A normal distribution will apply smaller\
    \ perturbations more frequently, allowing for a finer-grained search around the initial high-quality grid points, while still occasionally applying larger perturbations to escape the immediate vicinity. This may be a more efficient way to explore the local landscape around each seed.\n\nI will adapt my SOTA replication script to use `np.random.normal` for the jitter and resubmit. I will also increase the number of multi-starts slightly, as this change is computationally cheap.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Innovation: Gaussian Jitter Seeding\"\ntags: \"innovation, slsqp, seeding-strategy, jitter, sota-push\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment builds on the SOTA replication by modifying the jittering process in the seeding stage. Instead of a uniform random perturbation, this submission uses a Gaussian (normal) distribution for the jitter. The hypothesis is that a normal distribution will more effectively explore the local landscape around the high-quality grid seed points\
    \ by prioritizing smaller, more precise perturbations while still allowing for occasional larger jumps. The number of starts is also increased to broaden the search.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import math\n\n  N = 26\n  R_MIN = 1e-6\n  SAFETY = 1e-9\n\n  def cr_to_z(centers, radii):\n      z = np.zeros(N * 3)\n      z[0::3] = centers[:, 0]; z[1::3] = centers[:, 1]; z[2::3] = radii\n      return z\n\n  def z_to_cr(z):\n      centers = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T\n      radii = z[2::3]\n      return centers, radii\n\n  def _generate_seed_gaussian_jitter(hole_cx, hole_cy, jitter_std, seed):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      coords = [0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9]\n      grid_centers = np.array([[x, y] for x in coords for y in coords])\n      hole_center = np.array([hole_cx, hole_cy])\n      base_centers = np.vstack([grid_centers, hole_center])\n      \n      # Using Gaussian (normal) jitter instead of uniform\n      jitter = np.random.normal(0,\
    \ jitter_std, size=base_centers.shape)\n      perturbed_centers = np.clip(base_centers + jitter, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      \n      initial_radii = np.full(N, 0.05)\n      return cr_to_z(perturbed_centers, initial_radii)\n\n  def objective(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n  def objective_jac(z):\n      g = np.zeros_like(z); g[2::3] = -1.0; return g\n\n  def ineq_boundary(z):\n      x, y, r = z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3]\n      return np.concatenate([x - r, 1.0 - x - r, y - r, 1.0 - y - r])\n  def jac_boundary(z):\n      J = np.zeros((4*N, 3*N));\n      for i in range(N):\n          xi, yi, ri = 3*i, 3*i+1, 3*i+2\n          J[i, xi] = 1.0; J[i, ri] = -1.0\n          J[N+i, xi] = -1.0; J[N+i, ri] = -1.0\n          J[2*N+i, yi] = 1.0; J[2*N+i, ri] = -1.0\n          J[3*N+i, yi] = -1.0; J[3*N+i, ri] = -1.0\n      return J\n\n  def ineq_nonoverlap(z):\n      centers, r = z_to_cr(z)\n      diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n      dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n      iu = np.triu_indices(N,\
    \ 1)\n      return dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n  def jac_nonoverlap(z):\n      centers, _ = z_to_cr(z)\n      m = N*(N-1)//2; J = np.zeros((m, 3*N))\n      k = 0\n      for i in range(N):\n          for j in range(i+1, N):\n              xi, yi, ri = 3*i, 3*i+1, 3*i+2\n              xj, yj, rj = 3*j, 3*j+1, 3*j+2\n              dx = centers[i,0] - centers[j,0]; dy = centers[i,1] - centers[j,1]\n              d = math.hypot(dx, dy)\n              invd = 1.0 / d if d > 1e-12 else 0.0\n              J[k, xi] = dx * invd; J[k, yi] = dy * invd; J[k, ri] = -1.0\n              J[k, xj] = -dx * invd; J[k, yj] = -dy * invd; J[k, rj] = -1.0\n              k += 1\n      return J\n\n  def _ensure_validity(centers, radii):\n      for i in range(N): radii[i] = min(radii[i], centers[i,0], 1-centers[i,0], centers[i,1], 1-centers[i,1])\n      for _ in range(10):\n          violations = 0\n          for i in range(N):\n              for j in range(i + 1, N):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i]\
    \ - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum:\n                      violations += 1\n                      if rad_sum > 0: scale = (dist / rad_sum) * (1.0-SAFETY); radii[i] *= scale; radii[j] *= scale\n          if violations == 0: break\n      return centers, np.maximum(radii, 0)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      hole_locations = [(0.2,0.2), (0.2,0.4), (0.4,0.2), (0.4,0.4), (0.6,0.6), (0.8,0.8), (0.8,0.6), (0.6,0.8)]\n      # The standard deviation will be scaled from the amplitude\n      jitter_amplitudes = [1.5e-3, 2.5e-3, 3.5e-3, 5.0e-3, 7.0e-3, 8.5e-3] # Added one more level\n      \n      best_score = -1\n      best_packing = (None, None)\n      \n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(N): bounds.extend([(0,1), (0,1), (R_MIN, 0.5)])\n      constraints = [\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_boundary, 'jac': jac_boundary},\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_nonoverlap, 'jac': jac_nonoverlap},\n     \
    \ ]\n\n      run_idx = 0\n      for hole_cx, hole_cy in hole_locations:\n          for jitter_amp in jitter_amplitudes:\n              run_idx += 1\n              seed = int(hole_cx*1000 + hole_cy*1000 + jitter_amp*10000) + run_idx + 2000 # Offset seed\n              # Standard deviation is often 1/3 to 1/2 of the uniform range for similar spread\n              jitter_std = jitter_amp / 2 \n              initial_z = _generate_seed_gaussian_jitter(hole_cx, hole_cy, jitter_std, seed)\n              \n              res = minimize(objective, initial_z, method='SLSQP', jac=objective_jac,\n                             bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints,\n                             options={'maxiter': 500, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n              \n              if res.success and -res.fun > best_score:\n                  best_score = -res.fun\n                  centers, radii = z_to_cr(res.x)\n                  best_packing = (centers, radii)\n\n      if best_packing[0] is None:\n          return\
    \ None, None\n\n      final_centers, final_radii = _ensure_validity(best_packing[0], best_packing[1])\n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
