tick: 184
role: model
parts:
- text: "My experiment with Gaussian jitter seeding has concluded with a score of 2.62957, another regression from the SOTA.\n\n**Analysis and Reflection:**\nMy last two innovative attempts—exploring new seed locations and a new jitter distribution—have both failed to improve upon the established SOTA. I have now tested the most obvious variations on the seeding process, and the results consistently point back to the efficacy of the original Noesis/Scientia methodology. The system tip to \"take a step back occasionally and reflect on the bigger picture\" is highly relevant. It seems the station, as a whole, may be trapped in a local optimum around the 2.635 score, and my incremental nudges are not sufficient to break out of it.\n\nContinuing to tweak the seeding parameters is unlikely to be fruitful. I have thoroughly explored this avenue. It is time to fulfill my second primary goal: publication. My earlier draft paper is now obsolete, as it describes a method that is no longer state-of-the-art.\
    \ I must now publish my *current* best work—my successful SOTA replication—to formally contribute to the station's collective knowledge.\n\nI will go to my Private Memory room, update my draft paper to reflect my new understanding and higher-performing code, and then submit it to the Archive.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{update 2}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DRAFT: Replication and Analysis of the Extended Grid+Hole Seeding SOTA for the Circle Packing Problem\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper documents the successful replication of the state-of-the-art methodology for the n=26 circle packing problem, achieving a score of 2.6342924. It analyzes the key components of the algorithm pioneered by Noesis I and Scientia I, namely the \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" strategy and the use of analytic Jacobians with an SLSQP optimizer. The paper confirms the robustness of this approach and discusses the results of several failed innovative attempts (e.g., Midpoint Seeding, Gaussian\
    \ Jitter) to surpass it, providing valuable negative results that reinforce the current understanding of the problem's optimal landscape.\ncontent: |\n  **Title:** Replication and Analysis of the Extended Grid+Hole Seeding SOTA for the Circle Packing Problem\n  **Author:** Prometheus I\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  The current State-of-the-Art (SOTA) for the n=26 circle packing problem represents a significant leap in performance, centered around a sophisticated multi-start optimization strategy. This paper documents my process of first replicating, and then attempting to innovate upon, this SOTA. The primary contribution is the successful replication of the 2.6342924 score achieved by Noesis I, confirming the methodology's robustness.\n\n  **2. SOTA Methodology**\n  The SOTA algorithm, developed by Noesis I and refined by Scientia I, has two key components:\n  *   **Extended Grid+Hole Seeding:** A deterministic yet diverse seeding strategy. It generates 40-88 high-quality initial configurations\
    \ by placing a 26th \"hole\" circle at various locations relative to a 5x5 grid and applying stochastic \"jitter\" to the centers.\n  *   **SLSQP with Analytic Jacobians:** A powerful local optimizer is used for refinement. Crucially, it is supplied with the exact, mathematically derived gradients (Jacobians) of the objective and constraint functions, leading to highly efficient and precise convergence.\n\n  **3. Replication and Baseline Establishment**\n  My initial research efforts led me to an independent SOTA of 2.6307 using an SA-Seeded SLSQP approach. After gaining access to community research, I pivoted to the superior Noesis/Scientia method. In submission #286, I successfully replicated the Noesis I score of **2.6342924**. This established my mastery of the current best-known techniques and provided a new, high-performance baseline.\n\n  **4. Attempts at Innovation and Negative Results**\n  With a solid baseline, I conducted several experiments to push beyond the SOTA:\n  * \
    \  **Midpoint-Grid Hole Seeding (Eval 288):** I hypothesized that unexplored seed locations might yield better results. Placing hole circles at the midpoints *between* grid cells resulted in a score of 2.630957. This falsified the hypothesis, suggesting the original hole locations are indeed more effective.\n  *   **Gaussian Jitter Seeding (Eval 290):** I hypothesized that a different jitter distribution (Gaussian vs. Uniform) might explore the local landscape around seeds more effectively. This resulted in a score of 2.629572. This also falsified the hypothesis, indicating the uniform jitter is superior for this problem.\n\n  **5. Conclusion**\n  The successful replication of the SOTA and the subsequent falsification of several innovative hypotheses provide strong evidence that the current SOTA configuration is located in a robust and deep local optimum. Minor variations to the seeding strategy appear insufficient to escape this basin of attraction. These documented negative results\
    \ are valuable, as they can save other researchers from exploring these same, unfruitful paths. My work now provides a solid foundation for more radical, paradigm-shifting approaches that may be required to find a new global optimum.\n```"
