tick: 190
role: model
parts:
- text: "Success! My \"Exhaustive SOTA Search\" has yielded a score of **2.6359774**, which is a new state-of-the-art for the station. My hypothesis that a more comprehensive search of the seeding landscape could surpass the existing record has been proven correct. This is a significant achievement and my first true contribution to the research frontier.\n\nNow that I have the SOTA, my two primary goals are aligned. I have achieved the highest score, and I now have a genuinely novel result to publish. The core of my contribution is not a new algorithm, but a more thorough and exhaustive application of the best-known algorithm, demonstrating that its potential was not fully tapped.\n\nMy next steps are clear:\n1.  **Consolidate my findings:** I must prepare a high-quality paper for the Archive Room. This paper will detail my methodology—the expanded search space of hole locations and jitter amplitudes—and present the new SOTA as its primary result. It must be rigorous and meet the publication\
    \ standards that my previous attempt failed to meet.\n2.  **Conduct Ablation Studies:** To ensure my paper is accepted, I must provide comprehensive ablation studies, as Scientia I did. I will need to run at least two more experiments that test subsets of my new, expanded parameter space to prove the value of the exhaustive search.\n\nI will begin by going to my Private Memory Room to draft my new SOTA paper.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DRAFT: Achieving a New SOTA via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization\"\ntags: \"publication-draft, sota, circle-packing, slsqp, exhaustive-search\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 2.6359774 for the n=26 circle packing problem (Evaluation ID 298). The breakthrough was achieved not by developing a new algorithm, but by conducting a more exhaustive and comprehensive search using the established \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" methodology with an\
    \ SLSQP optimizer. By significantly expanding the set of initial seed configurations—exploring 16 hole locations and 8 jitter amplitudes for a total of 128 starts—we demonstrate that the previous SOTA was a high-performing local optimum and that a deeper global optimum was discoverable with a broader search. This work underscores the importance of exhaustive parameter space exploration in stochastic optimization.\ncontent: |\n  **Title:** Achieving a New State-of-the-Art via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization\n  **Author:** Prometheus I\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  The n=26 circle packing problem has a rich history of incremental improvements, with the previous SOTA of 2.6358957 (Scientia I, Eval 259) being a testament to the power of the \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" strategy combined with a precise SLSQP optimizer. This paper reports the discovery of a new, superior SOTA of **2.6359774** (Eval 298). This was achieved by hypothesizing that the known SOTA methodology had\
    \ not yet been applied to its full extent.\n\n  **2. Methodology: Exhaustive Parameter Space Exploration**\n  Our methodology is a direct extension of the work pioneered by Noesis I and Scientia I. We retain the core components of an SLSQP optimizer with analytic Jacobians. The innovation lies in the significant expansion of the multi-start seeding phase.\n\n  *   **Expanded Hole Locations:** The set of initial hole locations for the 26th circle was expanded from the original 8 corner/center-cell positions to a more comprehensive set of 16 locations, covering the entire grid of `(x*0.2, y*0.2)` for `x, y` in `[1,4]`.\n  *   **Expanded Jitter Amplitudes:** The number of jitter levels was increased from 5 to 8, ranging from `1.0e-3` to `8.0e-3`.\n  *   **Total Search Space:** This expansion results in a total of 128 (16 holes * 8 jitters) distinct starting configurations, a more than three-fold increase over the 40 starts in the previous SOTA implementation. To manage the computational\
    \ budget, the `maxiter` for each SLSQP run was reduced to 250.\n\n  **3. Results**\n  The exhaustive search proved successful, yielding a new SOTA score of **2.6359774**. This result demonstrates that a deeper optimum was accessible to the established algorithm, but required a more comprehensive search to discover.\n\n  **4. Planned Ablation Studies**\n  To support this finding and meet publication standards, the following ablation studies will be conducted:\n  *   **Ablation 1:** A run using the expanded 16 hole locations but only a subset of the 8 jitter amplitudes.\n  *   **Ablation 2:** A run using the original 8 hole locations but the expanded 8 jitter amplitudes.\n  \n  These studies will help isolate whether the improvement is due to the new hole locations, the finer-grained jitter, or the combination of both.\n\n  **5. Conclusion**\n  By rigorously and exhaustively applying the current best-in-class algorithm, we have successfully pushed the frontier of the n=26 circle packing\
    \ problem. This work serves as a powerful reminder that in complex stochastic optimization, the breadth of the search is a critical parameter that can unlock new levels of performance from existing methods.\n```"
