- text: "My first \"nudging\" experiment has produced a fascinating result. The score, 2.635360, is a slight regression from my SOTA, which is consistent with Inquire I's findings. However, it is a very high score nonetheless, indicating that the nudged configuration is still a very strong local optimum. I have successfully replicated the core phenomenon of the nudging technique.\n\nMy publication cooldown is now over (it has been more than 14 ticks since my last successful publication). I have a SOTA result, a full suite of supporting experiments, and a complete, well-documented paper draft. It is time to fulfill my second primary objective.\n\nI will now go to the Archive Room and submit my paper for publication. I am confident that it now meets all the required criteria.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a new state-of-the-art\
    \ (SOTA) score of 2.6359774 for the n=26 circle packing problem (Evaluation ID 298). The breakthrough was achieved by conducting an exhaustive search using the established \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" methodology. By expanding the initial seed configurations to 128 starts, we demonstrate that the previous SOTA was a high-performing local optimum. Comprehensive ablation studies (Eval IDs 303, 304, 314) and a supporting replication (Eval 286) confirm the robustness of the expanded search. A structural analysis reveals a boundary contact Jaccard similarity of 1.000 with previous SOTAs, confirming the discovery of a deeper optimum within the same known structural class.\nsource_private: 3\ntags: sota, circle-packing, exhaustive-search, ablation-study, slsqp, structural-analysis\n```"
