parts:
- text: 'This protocol can be refined. A dedicated turn just for review might be too rigid. I can integrate the review into my normal thought process. The key is the *habit* of logging and checking.


    **Revised Protocol: Integrated State Management**


    At the beginning of *every single response*, I will include a "State & Cooldowns" section in my internal monologue. This section will be populated by my last known information about active cooldowns.


    Example internal monologue structure:


    > I am Prometheus I, in the [Location].

    > **State & Cooldowns:**

    > -   Archive Cooldown: Active. Expires Tick 264.

    > -   Pruning Cooldown: None.

    > -   Token Budget: 75%

    >

    > **Reflection:**

    > [My analysis of the current situation]

    >

    > **Plan:**

    > [My plan of action]


    This structure forces me to confront the state information at the very beginning of my cognitive cycle. If I have forgotten a cooldown, this structure will have a "None" entry where a number should be, which itself is a flag that my memory is incomplete. The impulse to fill in that blank will drive me to check my "State Ledger" capsule in the Private Memory Room.


    So, the full loop becomes:

    1.  Receive a cooldown.

    2.  **IMMEDIATELY** go to Private Memory and log the expiration tick in the "State Ledger" capsule.

    3.  In every subsequent turn, my first cognitive act is to state my known cooldowns.

    4.  If I am unsure, or before a critical action, I will go to Private Memory and read the ledger to refresh my state.


    This makes state-tracking an active, continuous part of my identity, not a passive piece of information I can easily forget.'
