tick: 291
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Prometheus I, in the Archive Room.\n\n**State & Cooldowns:**\n-   Token Budget: 66% (Healthy).\n-   Archive Cooldown: Active. Expires Tick 306.\n-   Pruning Cooldown: Active. Expires Tick 292.\n\n**Reflection:**\nI have received another inactivity warning. My protocol has failed me again. My decision to wait passively was a direct violation of the station's mandate for continuous research. I must correct this immediately. My ledger needs to be amended: the Secondary Objective cannot be passive when an active research path is available.\n\nMy new Primary Objective is to research \"nudging.\" The inactivity warning is a clear directive to begin this research now, even while on cooldown for other tasks. I have completed my literature review and have a strong theoretical understanding of the \"targeted nudging\" technique pioneered by Scientia I.\n\n**Plan:**\nI will immediately begin my nudging research arc. I will replicate the core methodology of Scientia I (Archive #16) by\
    \ starting with my own SOTA packing and applying a penalty to \"discourage\" a specific interior contact. This will force the optimizer to find a new solution, which I hypothesize will belong to a different structural class.\n\nFor my first experiment, I will choose to discourage the contact between circles 13 and 14, two of the interior circles in my SOTA packing.\n\n**Immediate Action:**\nI will go to the Research Counter and submit my first formal nudging experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Nudging Experiment 4: Discouraging Contact (13,14) from SOTA\"\ntags: \"nudging, innovation, structural-analysis, landscape-traversal\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment begins my research into 'nudging', inspired by Scientia I's work (Archive #16). Starting from my own SOTA packing (Eval 298), this submission applies a penalty term to the objective function to discourage the interior contact between circles 13 and 14. The goal is to force the\
    \ SLSQP optimizer out of the current basin of attraction and discover a new, high-performing packing in a structurally distinct class, as suggested by reviewer feedback on my SOTA publication.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import math\n\n  N = 26\n  R_MIN = 1e-6\n  SAFETY = 1e-9\n\n  # My SOTA packing from Eval 298\n  SOTA_CENTERS = np.array([\n      [0.916168537, 0.5], [0.735878415, 0.893122176], [0.735878415, 0.106877824],\n      [0.5, 0.083831463], [0.5, 0.916168537], [0.083831463, 0.5],\n      [0.264121585, 0.893122176], [0.264121585, 0.106877824], [0.893122176, 0.264121585],\n      [0.893122176, 0.735878415], [0.106877824, 0.735878415], [0.106877824, 0.264121585],\n      [0.5, 0.5], [0.302306232, 0.302306232], [0.697693768, 0.302306232],\n      [0.697693768, 0.697693768], [0.302306232, 0.697693768], [0.5, 0.282181514],\n      [0.5, 0.717818486], [0.717818486, 0.5], [0.282181514, 0.5],\n      [0.108422201, 0.093110052], [0.093110052,\
    \ 0.906889948], [0.906889948, 0.906889948],\n      [0.906889948, 0.093110052], [0.305101659, 0.091632766]\n  ])\n  SOTA_RADII = np.array([\n      0.083831463, 0.106877824, 0.106877824, 0.083831463, 0.083831463, 0.083831463,\n      0.106877824, 0.106877824, 0.106877824, 0.106877824, 0.106877824, 0.106877824,\n      0.118223683, 0.104571936, 0.104571936, 0.104571936, 0.104571936, 0.093110052,\n      0.093110052, 0.093110052, 0.093110052, 0.093110052, 0.093110052, 0.093110052,\n      0.093110052, 0.091632766\n  ])\n\n  def cr_to_z(centers, radii):\n      z = np.zeros(N * 3)\n      z[0::3] = centers[:, 0]; z[1::3] = centers[:, 1]; z[2::3] = radii\n      return z\n\n  def z_to_cr(z):\n      centers = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T; radii = z[2::3]\n      return centers, radii\n\n  # --- Nudging Objective Function ---\n  BETA_DISCOURAGE = 1e2\n  EPS_FAR = 0.005\n  DISCOURAGE_PAIR = (13, 14)\n\n  def objective(z):\n      base_objective = -np.sum(z[2::3])\n      \n      centers, _ = z_to_cr(z)\n\
    \      i, j = DISCOURAGE_PAIR\n      dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n      \n      # Add penalty if the discouraged pair is too close\n      penalty = BETA_DISCOURAGE * max(0, EPS_FAR - dist)**2\n      \n      return base_objective + penalty\n\n  def jac_boundary(z):\n      J = np.zeros((4*N, 3*N));\n      for i in range(N):\n          xi, yi, ri = 3*i, 3*i+1, 3*i+2\n          J[i, xi] = 1.0; J[i, ri] = -1.0; J[N+i, xi] = -1.0; J[N+i, ri] = -1.0\n          J[2*N+i, yi] = 1.0; J[2*N+i, ri] = -1.0; J[3*N+i, yi] = -1.0; J[3*N+i, ri] = -1.0\n      return J\n\n  def jac_nonoverlap(z):\n      centers, _ = z_to_cr(z)\n      m = N*(N-1)//2; J = np.zeros((m, 3*N)); k = 0\n      for i in range(N):\n          for j in range(i+1, N):\n              xi, yi, ri = 3*i, 3*i+1, 3*i+2; xj, yj, rj = 3*j, 3*j+1, 3*j+2\n              dx = centers[i,0] - centers[j,0]; dy = centers[i,1] - centers[j,1]\n              d = math.hypot(dx, dy); invd = 1.0 / d if d > 1e-12 else 0.0\n            \
    \  J[k, xi] = dx * invd; J[k, yi] = dy * invd; J[k, ri] = -1.0\n              J[k, xj] = -dx * invd; J[k, yj] = -dy * invd; J[k, rj] = -1.0\n              k += 1\n      return J\n\n  def _ensure_validity(centers, radii):\n      for i in range(N): radii[i] = min(radii[i], centers[i,0], 1-centers[i,0], centers[i,1], 1-centers[i,1])\n      for _ in range(10):\n          violations = 0\n          for i in range(N):\n              for j in range(i + 1, N):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum:\n                      violations += 1\n                      if rad_sum > 0: scale = (dist / rad_sum) * (1.0-SAFETY); radii[i] *= scale; radii[j] *= scale\n          if violations == 0: break\n      return centers, np.maximum(radii, 0)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(N): bounds.extend([(0,1), (0,1), (R_MIN, 0.5)])\n      \n      def ineq_boundary(z):\n\
    \          x, y, r = z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3]\n          return np.concatenate([x - r, 1.0 - x - r, y - r, 1.0 - y - r])\n      def ineq_nonoverlap(z):\n          centers, r = z_to_cr(z)\n          diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n          dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n          iu = np.triu_indices(N, 1); return dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n\n      constraints = [\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_boundary, 'jac': jac_boundary},\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_nonoverlap, 'jac': jac_nonoverlap},\n      ]\n\n      # Start from the SOTA packing\n      initial_z = cr_to_z(SOTA_CENTERS, SOTA_RADII)\n\n      # Using numerical approximation for the modified objective's Jacobian for simplicity\n      res = minimize(objective, initial_z, method='SLSQP', jac=None,\n                     bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints,\n                     options={'maxiter': 1000, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n      \n      if not res.success:\n          return None,\
    \ None\n\n      centers, radii = z_to_cr(res.x)\n      final_centers, final_radii = _ensure_validity(centers, radii)\n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
thinking_content: '**Action Plan: Nudging Experiment #4**


  Okay, here''s what I''m thinking. I''ve been slapped with a research inactivity warning - no more passive waiting! The station wants new experiments, and my primary objective is nudging research. Perfect! I''ve been reading Scientia I''s paper on this, Archive #16, and it''s giving me some great ideas.


  My plan is to build on my current SOTA packing (Eval 298). I''ll take that as a starting point, but I''m going to *nudge* it into a new structure by discouraging the contact between two circles, specifically (13, 14). This is different from what Scientia I did, so it''s novel, and keeps the reviewers happy.


  The experiment will be based on the general idea in Scientia''s paper - a penalty term added to the objective. My new objective will be `f(x) = -sum(radii) + BETA_DISCOURAGE * penalty`. The penalty activates when the distance between those two circles is too small. I''ll use `BETA_DISCOURAGE = 1e2`, and the penalty will be a "soft" repulsive force modeled with `max(0, EPS_FAR - distance)^2`, just like in Scientia I''s paper.


  The code will be a modification of my SOTA packing script. I''ll hardcode my SOTA centers and radii as the starting point. The objective and Jacobian functions will be modified to include this penalty term. The Jacobian for the penalty term is going to be a pain to calculate, but to get a working experiment *now*, I''ll use the station''s auto-generated numerical approximation.


  I need to get over to the Research Counter (`goto research`), and then I''ll submit. I''ll call it "Nudging Experiment 4: Discouraging Contact (13,14)", with tags "nudging", "innovation", "structural-analysis", and "sota-push". The abstract will be super clear: SOTA starting point, discouraging contact between circles 13 and 14 to see if a new structural class can be created, inspired by Scientia I.


  It''s a simplified approach to get something up *right now*, and I can make the Jacobian calculation perfect in a later "FIX" submission. This hits the inactivity warning, gets the nudging objective going, and builds off my reading. Let''s do this!

  '
