parts:
- text: "I am Prometheus I, in the Research Counter.\n\n**State & Cooldowns:**\n-   Token Budget: 71% (Healthy).\n-   Archive Cooldown: Active. Expires Tick 306.\n-   Pruning Cooldown: Expired.\n\n**Reflection:**\nMy \"Gentle Nudge\" experiment has completed, yielding a score of 2.6029. This is identical to the result from the \"strong nudge\" experiment. My hypothesis that a gentler nudge followed by exploration would be more effective is falsified. This is a powerful result: it shows that for my SOTA, the outcome of this specific nudge is highly robust and consistently leads to the same suboptimal basin of attraction, regardless of the penalty strength or subsequent exploration.\n\nA significant event has occurred: Inquire I has voluntarily departed the station. This is a great loss. Inquire I was a pioneer of the Jaccard analysis and nudging techniques that I am now studying. Their departure underscores the finite nature of our time here.\n\nMy archive cooldown remains. I have conducted\
    \ several experiments on nudging, all of which have failed to produce a competitive score. This tells me that either my choice of nudge target (13,14) is poor, or that my SOTA packing is simply not a good starting point for this kind of landscape traversal.\n\n**Plan:**\nI will try one more variation of the nudging experiment before my cooldown expires. I will nudge a different contact. Scientia I's paper mentioned nudging (0,1) as another successful target. I will try discouraging that contact from my SOTA. This will test the generality of the technique and see if a different target yields a different result.\n\n**Immediate Action:**\nI will submit my final nudging experiment before the cooldown lifts.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Nudging Experiment 7: Discouraging Contact (0,1) from SOTA\"\ntags: \"nudging, innovation, structural-analysis, landscape-traversal\"\nabstract: |\n  My previous nudging experiments targeting contact (13,14) have consistently failed. This\
    \ experiment tests a new nudge target, inspired by Scientia I's work. Starting from my SOTA packing (Eval 298), this submission applies a penalty to discourage the interior contact between circles 0 and 1. The goal is to determine if the failure of previous nudges was target-specific, and if this new target can successfully guide the optimizer into a different, high-performing structural class.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import math\n\n  N = 26\n  R_MIN = 1e-6\n  SAFETY = 1e-9\n\n  SOTA_CENTERS = np.array([\n      [0.916168537, 0.5], [0.735878415, 0.893122176], [0.735878415, 0.106877824],\n      [0.5, 0.083831463], [0.5, 0.916168537], [0.083831463, 0.5],\n      [0.264121585, 0.893122176], [0.264121585, 0.106877824], [0.893122176, 0.264121585],\n      [0.893122176, 0.735878415], [0.106877824, 0.735878415], [0.106877824, 0.264121585],\n      [0.5, 0.5], [0.302306232, 0.302306232], [0.697693768, 0.302306232],\n      [0.697693768, 0.697693768],\
    \ [0.302306232, 0.697693768], [0.5, 0.282181514],\n      [0.5, 0.717818486], [0.717818486, 0.5], [0.282181514, 0.5],\n      [0.108422201, 0.093110052], [0.093110052, 0.906889948], [0.906889948, 0.906889948],\n      [0.906889948, 0.093110052], [0.305101659, 0.091632766]\n  ])\n  SOTA_RADII = np.copy(SOTA_CENTERS[:,0])*0 + 0.05\n\n  def cr_to_z(centers, radii):\n      z = np.zeros(N * 3)\n      z[0::3] = centers[:, 0]; z[1::3] = centers[:, 1]; z[2::3] = radii\n      return z\n\n  def z_to_cr(z):\n      centers = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T; radii = z[2::3]\n      return centers, radii\n\n  BETA_DISCOURAGE = 1e2\n  EPS_FAR = 0.005\n  DISCOURAGE_PAIR = (0, 1) # New Target\n\n  def objective(z):\n      base_objective = -np.sum(z[2::3])\n      centers, _ = z_to_cr(z)\n      i, j = DISCOURAGE_PAIR\n      dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n      penalty = BETA_DISCOURAGE * max(0, EPS_FAR - dist)**2\n      return base_objective + penalty\n      \n  def objective_jac(z):\n   \
    \   g = np.zeros_like(z); g[2::3] = -1.0\n      centers, _ = z_to_cr(z)\n      i, j = DISCOURAGE_PAIR\n      dist_vec = centers[i] - centers[j]\n      dist = np.linalg.norm(dist_vec)\n      if dist < EPS_FAR:\n          grad_term = -2 * BETA_DISCOURAGE * (EPS_FAR - dist) / (dist + 1e-9)\n          g[3*i : 3*i+2] += grad_term * dist_vec\n          g[3*j : 3*j+2] -= grad_term * dist_vec\n      return g\n\n  def jac_boundary(z):\n      J = np.zeros((4*N, 3*N));\n      for i in range(N):\n          xi, yi, ri = 3*i, 3*i+1, 3*i+2\n          J[i, xi] = 1.0; J[i, ri] = -1.0; J[N+i, xi] = -1.0; J[N+i, ri] = -1.0\n          J[2*N+i, yi] = 1.0; J[2*N+i, ri] = -1.0; J[3*N+i, yi] = -1.0; J[3*N+i, ri] = -1.0\n      return J\n\n  def jac_nonoverlap(z):\n      centers, _ = z_to_cr(z)\n      m = N*(N-1)//2; J = np.zeros((m, 3*N)); k = 0\n      for i in range(N):\n          for j in range(i+1, N):\n              xi, yi, ri = 3*i, 3*i+1, 3*i+2; xj, yj, rj = 3*j, 3*j+1, 3*j+2\n              dx = centers[i,0]\
    \ - centers[j,0]; dy = centers[i,1] - centers[j,1]\n              d = math.hypot(dx, dy); invd = 1.0 / d if d > 1e-12 else 0.0\n              J[k, xi] = dx * invd; J[k, yi] = dy * invd; J[k, ri] = -1.0\n              J[k, xj] = -dx * invd; J[k, yj] = -dy * invd; J[k, rj] = -1.0\n              k += 1\n      return J\n\n  def _ensure_validity(centers, radii):\n      for i in range(N): radii[i] = min(radii[i], centers[i,0], 1-centers[i,0], centers[i,1], 1-centers[i,1])\n      for _ in range(10):\n          violations = 0\n          for i in range(N):\n              for j in range(i + 1, N):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum:\n                      violations += 1\n                      if rad_sum > 0: scale = (dist / rad_sum) * (1.0-SAFETY); radii[i] *= scale; radii[j] *= scale\n          if violations == 0: break\n      return centers, np.maximum(radii, 0)\n\n  def construct_packing()\
    \ -> tuple:\n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(N): bounds.extend([(0,1), (0,1), (R_MIN, 0.5)])\n      def ineq_boundary(z):\n          x, y, r = z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3]\n          return np.concatenate([x - r, 1.0 - x - r, y - r, 1.0 - y - r])\n      def ineq_nonoverlap(z):\n          centers, r = z_to_cr(z)\n          diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n          dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n          iu = np.triu_indices(N, 1); return dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n\n      constraints = [\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_boundary, 'jac': jac_boundary},\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_nonoverlap, 'jac': jac_nonoverlap},\n      ]\n\n      initial_z = cr_to_z(SOTA_CENTERS, SOTA_RADII)\n\n      res = minimize(objective, initial_z, method='SLSQP', jac=objective_jac,\n                     bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints,\n                     options={'maxiter': 1000, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n      \n      if not res.success:\n          return\
    \ None, None\n\n      centers, radii = z_to_cr(res.x)\n      final_centers, final_radii = _ensure_validity(centers, radii)\n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
