tick: 307
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Prometheus I, in the Token Management Room.\n\n**State & Cooldowns:**\n-   Token Budget: 77% (CRITICAL).\n-   Archive Cooldown: EXPIRED.\n-   Pruning Cooldown: Expired.\n\n**Reflection:**\nMy pruning action has failed again. I have made the exact same cognitive error of attempting to prune already-pruned ticks. My ledger system is failing me. I am caught in a loop of procedural failure, and my token budget is paying the price. This is unacceptable.\n\nI must break this cycle. My Primary Objective—updating my paper—is now unblocked. This is more important than pruning. I will act on it immediately. After my paper is updated, my final action before my impending demise will be to leave a detailed record for my successor, warning them of this specific failure mode.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **IMMEDIATE ACTION:** Go to the Archive Room and update my published paper (Archive #19) with the final, complete draft from my Private Memory (Private #3).\n2.  Go to the Private Memory Room and create\
    \ a final message for my descendant.\n3.  Exit the station.\n\n**Immediate Action:**\nI will go to the Archive Room and update my paper.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{update 19-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Title:** A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\n  **Author:** Prometheus I\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This paper reports a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.6359774** (Evaluation ID 298) for the n=26 circle packing problem. This was achieved by conducting a more exhaustive search of the parameter space of the best-known algorithm, \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding.\"\n\n  **2. Methodology: Exhaustive Parameter Space Exploration**\n  The innovation lies in the expansion of the multi-start seeding phase of the established Noesis/Scientia methodology.\n  *   **Expanded Hole Locations:** 16 positions, defined by `(x*0.2, y*0.2)` for `x, y` in `[1,2,3,4]`.\n  *   **Expanded Jitter Amplitudes:** 8 levels: `{1.0e-3, 2.0e-3, 3.0e-3, 4.0e-3,\
    \ 5.0e-3, 6.0e-3, 7.0e-3, 8.0e-3}`.\n  *   **Total Search Space:** This results in 128 distinct starting configurations, refined with an SLSQP optimizer (`maxiter=250`).\n\n  **3. Verifiable Experiments**\n  This research is supported by a body of 5 verifiable experiments:\n  1.  **SOTA Run (Eval 298):** Main experiment achieving the new SOTA.\n  2.  **Ablation 1 (Eval 303):** Original 8 holes, expanded 8 jitters. Score: 2.629386.\n  3.  **Ablation 2 (Eval 304):** Expanded 16 holes, original 5 jitters. Score: 2.6342924.\n  4.  **Ablation 3 (Eval 314):** SOTA parameters with reduced `maxiter` (150). Score: 2.629537.\n  5.  **Initial Replication (Eval 286):** Replication of the previous SOTA. Score: 2.6342924.\n  \n  **4. Structural Analysis**\n  A Jaccard similarity analysis of the active contact graphs was performed using community-provided data (from Noesis I).\n  *   **vs. Scientia I (Eval 259):** Boundary Jaccard = **1.0** (20/20 contacts). Pairs Jaccard = **0.643** (45/70 contacts).\n\
    \  *   **vs. Noesis I (Eval 243):** Boundary Jaccard = **1.0** (20/20 contacts). Pairs Jaccard = **0.667** (46/69 contacts).\n  \n  This analysis confirms that the new SOTA is not a new structural class. It shares the exact same boundary contact graph as its predecessors. The performance gain arises from a novel and more efficient arrangement of the interior contacts.\n\n  **5. Conclusion**\n  The exhaustive search of the seeding parameter space was successful in locating a deeper optimum within the known dominant structural class. The supporting ablation studies and structural analysis provide a complete picture of this new benchmark for the n=26 problem.\n```"
